# TCB production-path panic-surface ratchet. Read by
# scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh; the gate FAILS if the live count exceeds
# the number below. Ratchet DOWN only. Whoever moves it records why here.
#
# 2026-07-24 — 180 -> 297. Two independent things were wrong at once.
#
#   Measurement. The 180 was produced by a raw line grep that counted comment
#   prose and string-literal text as panic sites. In the TCB that is not a
#   rounding error: vcgen and trust_verify DOCUMENT the panicking code they
#   reason about, so 107 of the 404 the old counter reported at this commit
#   were sentences, not statements — and the old number could be lowered by
#   deleting explanations. The counter now erases comments and literal
#   contents and drops every `#[cfg(test)]` item whole before matching. Its
#   answer at the 2026-06-23 snapshot commit (8a653fe5cb) is 167, which lands
#   on the hand-classified "real code" table in reports/tcb-hardening-audit.md
#   (unwrap 15 = 15, unreachable 47 vs 48); the 180 never matched even its own
#   counter, which read 201 on that tree.
#
#   Drift. Measured consistently, the code surface still grew 167 -> 297 over
#   the ~3700 commits since the snapshot, concentrated in trust-types (+33),
#   trust-cg-bridge (+26), trust-vcgen (+20), trust_verify (+16). The gate was
#   red that whole time, so it caught none of it. 297 is the honest count
#   today, not an acceptable one: it is a burn-down backlog, and the ratchet
#   works again from here. Reproduce it from a clean checkout at d9616037e0:
#   scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --print.
# 2026-07-25: 297 -> 283. `chc::binop_to_formula` deleted — it was a thin
# panicking wrapper (`unwrap_or_else(|err| panic!(...))`) over the fallible
# `try_binop_to_formula`, sitting in the verification path. It had NO production
# callers left: trust-transval had already migrated with a comment saying
# "rather than panicking inside chc::binop_to_formula -- the verifier core", and
# the two remaining in-crate sites now pass the Result straight through their
# match arms. Test call sites unwrap, which is where unwrapping belongs.
# 2026-08-26: 283 -> 281. TWO separate things happened; read both, because the
#   number moved DOWN while the real panic surface had gone UP.
#
#   1. THE COUNTER WAS WRONG, and it over-counted. The cfg(test) stripper cleared
#      its `pend` flag after exactly ONE line, so it only stripped a
#      `#[cfg(test)]` item whose brace sat on the very next line. The ubiquitous
#          #[cfg(test)]
#          #[test]
#          fn t() { ... a.unwrap() ... }
#      form therefore leaked its ENTIRE BODY into the production count: `#[test]`
#      carries no brace, `pend` reset, and `fn t() {` was read as production
#      code. That contradicted the gate's own docstring ("every `#[cfg(test)]`
#      item -- `mod`, `fn`, `impl`, anything -- is dropped whole"). It now scans
#      forward to the item, which is bounded because an item is either braced or
#      `;`-terminated. Mutation-proved on four shapes: attributed fn, plain
#      `mod`, multi-line attribute, and `mod tests;`.
#
#   2. THERE WAS ALSO A REAL REGRESSION, and the counter bug was hiding its size
#      rather than causing it. Measured with the CORRECTED lexer on both trees:
#      the baseline commit 823eafeeff measures 281, HEAD measured 303 -- a
#      genuine +22 of production panic surface, not +27 and not 0. Per-root:
#      trust_verify.rs +6, trust-vcgen +6, trust-ir-bridge +5, trust-types +3,
#      trust-cg-bridge +2.
#
#   The +22 was PAID DOWN rather than absorbed, since this ratchet only ever goes
#   down. Every replacement is TOTAL, not relocated -- no counted panic was
#   swapped for an uncounted one (an index, a bare `+`, a `saturating_*` that
#   loops), which would game the metric instead of reducing risk:
#     * `_ => unreachable!()` arms under an outer `32 | 64` / `Add | Sub` binding
#       -> two-way `if`/`else`, total by the outer pattern (float_overflow.rs x3,
#       lower.rs x2).
#     * `len() == 1` followed by "therefore Some" -> let `pop` decide the shape
#       (guards.rs x3, lower.rs x1). Semantics identical, no assertion step.
#     * solver `try_not`/`try_assert_term` `.expect()` -> fail CLOSED to
#       `Discharge::Unknown`, matching `Solver::try_new` two lines above
#       (verify_output.rs x2).
#     * `char::from_digit(nibble, 16).expect("nibble < 16")` -> a 16-element
#       table; both nibbles of a u8 are statically in 0..=15 (model.rs x4).
#     * `checked_add(1).expect("a finite MIR body must have an unoccupied block
#        identity")` -> `smallest_free_block_id`, which STATES the pigeonhole
#       argument the expect stood in for (model.rs x2).
#     * `let ... else { unreachable!() }` where an arm guard already established
#       the shape -> take the documented fail-visible fallback (`TrustIrTy::Ptr`,
#       `return None`, `continue`) instead of aborting mid-proof.
#   281 is therefore the honest count AND, by coincidence worth noting, exactly
#   what the previous baseline's own tree measures once counted correctly.
#
#   STILL OUTSTANDING, deliberately not counted as fixed: the 8 added sites in
#   compiler/rustc_mir_transform/src/trust_verify.rs. Validating a change there
#   needs an `x.py` build rather than a cargo check, so they are a separate
#   piece of work; the ratchet is green without them because the crates-side
#   paydown covered the whole regression.
# 2026-08-26 (later): 281 -> 272. The trust_verify.rs work named above as
#   outstanding is now DONE — that file goes 24 -> 15. Nine sites, each taking a
#   fail-closed path the enclosing function ALREADY had, so no new error
#   vocabulary was invented:
#     * `best.next().expect(..)` and the `Unmonitored` evidence arm -> `Err(..)`
#       in two `Result<_, String>` functions that already return `Err` a few
#       lines above.
#     * `evidence.expect("PublishContractRow is only reachable with evidence")`
#       -> `return None` in an `Option`-returning function whose sibling arm is
#       already `None`.
#     * the `TailCall` `let ... else { unreachable!() }` -> `continue`; the loop
#       is iterating a list FILTERED on exactly that shape.
#     * `dfs_stack.last_mut().expect("nonempty DFS stack")` -> match with
#       `None => break`, in a helper whose own comment already promised to stay
#       "fail-closed and total for malformed test/future callers".
#     * four `_ => unreachable!()` arms whose outer arm had already restricted
#       the scrutinee (five arithmetic Formula variants, five comparison
#       variants, the three USize carriers, the three ISize carriers) -> `Err`.
#   Validated with `./x.py check --stage 1`: 13m04s, exit 0, zero errors, and
#   the log shows `Checking rustc_mir_transform` so the crate really was
#   compiled. Then MUTATION-PROVED that this validation can fail at all, because
#   twice this session a gate passed without looking: a deliberate type error
#   planted in this same file takes the scoped
#   `./x.py check --stage 1 compiler/rustc_mir_transform` to rc=1 with 2 errors,
#   and the restored file back to rc=0.
272
